        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Mercantilism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1500 CE – 1846 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Thinkers</span><span class="info-stat-val">Thomas Mun, English merchant, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a> took as the system’s chief creator; Gerard de Malynes and Josiah Child in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>; Antonio Serra and Giovanni Botero in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-bodin">Jean Bodin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert">Jean-Baptiste Colbert</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>; Philipp Wilhelm von Hornick, a pioneer of cameralism in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>; James Steuart, author of perhaps the last major mercantilist work</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Key texts</span><span class="info-stat-val">Discourse of the Common Wealth of this Realm of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> (1549); Antonio Serra, A Short Treatise on the Wealth and Poverty of Nations (1613); Thomas Mun, England’s Treasure by Foreign Trade (published 1664); Philipp Wilhelm von Hornick, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> Over All, If She Only Will (1684); James Steuart, Principles of Political Economy (1767)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Core ideas</span><span class="info-stat-val">a nation’s wealth measured in gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> (bullionism); trade as a zero-sum game; a positive balance of trade, maximizing exports and minimizing imports; high tariffs, especially on manufactured goods; colonies as sources of raw materials and exclusive markets; state regulation of the economy to strengthen the state against its rivals; a large working population kept at the margins of subsistence</span></div>
