    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Mercantilism held that a nation grows rich and strong by exporting more than it imports and amassing gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, and it was the dominant school of economic thought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> through the early modern period.</li><li>Treating trade as a zero-sum game, it was bound up with war and empire: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-dutch-wars">Anglo-Dutch Wars</a> can be linked directly to its theories, and it fuelled the conquest of colonies, settler colonialism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</li><li>Undermined by Locke and Hume and rebutted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-wealth-of-nations">The Wealth of Nations</a> in 1776, it gave way to free trade in the 19th century, though protectionist policies are still called neo-mercantilist.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Mercantilism was a name given by its critics: Mirabeau used the word before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a> wrote of the “mercantile system”, and it came into English from German only in the early 19th century, long after most of the writers it describes.</p></aside>
    </section>

